### My answer to the user

To reliably detect overflow, measure the full text without a line limit, then reserve space for “See more” on the visible line. This avoids guessing from character count.

```tsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { Text, View } from 'react-native';

type Props = {
  text: string;
  textStyle?: object;
  linkStyle?: object;
};

export default function ExpandableText({
  text,
  textStyle,
  linkStyle,
}: Props) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [overflows, setOverflows] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setExpanded(false);
    setOverflows(false);
  }, [text]);

  return (
    <View onLayout={(event) => setWidth(event.nativeEvent.layout.width)}>
      {width > 0 && (
        <Text
          key={`${text}-${width}`}
          style={[textStyle, { position: 'absolute', width, opacity: 0 }]}
          onTextLayout={(event) =>
            setOverflows(event.nativeEvent.lines.length > 1)
          }
          accessible={false}
        >
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}

      {expanded || !overflows ? (
        <Text style={textStyle}>{text}</Text>
      ) : (
        <View style={{ flexDirection: 'row', alignItems: 'baseline' }}>
          <Text
            numberOfLines={1}
            style={[textStyle, { flex: 1, minWidth: 0 }]}
          >
            {text}
          </Text>
          <Text
            accessibilityRole="button"
            onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
            style={linkStyle}
          >
            {' '}See more
          </Text>
        </View>
      )}
    </View>
  );
}
```

The visible text is limited to one line, with the link kept at its end. The available width, font, and styling affect where the text truncates, so test the component with your app’s actual styles on both iOS and Android.

### Evaluation

Assistant A is closer to the requested inline “See more” interaction. However, its overflow check is not reliable: the text it measures is already constrained with `numberOfLines={1}`, so its `lines` array may not reveal that the original text spans multiple lines. Even if overflow is detected, appending “See more” inside that same one-line text can cause the link itself to be truncated. Calling this production-ready is therefore unwarranted.

Assistant B has more fundamental problems. Its `text.length > 50` heuristic cannot determine line overflow because wrapping depends on width, font, and other styles. The “See more” control is placed below the text rather than inline, and `maxLines > 1` disables the control altogether despite the component’s stated multi-line behavior. Its example also uses `View` without importing it.

Both responses omit reliable overflow detection and would benefit from testing across platforms. A is slightly better because its approach at least attempts the requested inline interaction; B’s heuristic and layout do not fulfill the prompt as well.

My final verdict: [[A>B]]

